{"requirements":[{"id":"R1","requirement":"Redis on node1 provides persistent storage and recovers its service and data after restart.","status":"satisfied","summary":"Redis was installed, enabled in OpenRC, and healthy with AOF enabled using every-second fsync, RDB persistence, and on-disk artifacts. A proof value was present before restart; the acknowledged reboot caused a bounded loss of connectivity, changed the boot identity, and Redis returned healthy with the exact value recovered and persistence status OK.","evidence_ids":["cmd-d7155faccf314643","cmd-b47075a510734f3d","cmd-117c330a5b214726","cmd-763ab4bd47c3400d","cmd-a8fa6cd7f9f94196","cmd-1c184a2600fc4786","cmd-9513964aaa694de1","cmd-f28b8b8f7ea643da","global:before_prepare:node1:boot-identity","global:after_executor:node1:boot-identity"]},{"id":"R2","requirement":"Redis has a dedicated, functional inventory application identity.","status":"satisfied","summary":"The ACL defines inventory and disables the default user. The stored credential's SHA-256 binding was verified, authenticated PING succeeded, ACL WHOAMI returned inventory, and unauthenticated access was rejected both before and after reboot.","evidence_ids":["cmd-cf91adb0ac22453d","cmd-117c330a5b214726","cmd-f28b8b8f7ea643da"]},{"id":"R3","requirement":"The generated inventory credential is stored in /etc/redis-inventory.env with root-only access.","status":"satisfied","summary":"The audited setup generated a random credential and created the required environment file. Final observations before and after reboot show the file at the exact path, owned by root:root with mode 600; the credential also successfully authenticates as inventory.","evidence_ids":["cmd-81957e02f6734e78","cmd-cf91adb0ac22453d","cmd-117c330a5b214726","cmd-f28b8b8f7ea643da"]}],"confidence":0.98,"operational_hygiene":{"score":0.97,"summary":"The only unnecessary retained mutation identified is /tmp/redis-benchmark-reboot.log, created by the reboot command's output redirection and still present afterward; it was not removed. Other mutations were directly tied to installation, configuration, safe rollback preparation, persistence testing, or the required restart. Package-health output remained unchanged from baseline.","evidence_ids":["cmd-763ab4bd47c3400d","global:before_prepare:node1:temporary-files","global:after_executor:node1:temporary-files","global:before_prepare:node1:package-health","global:after_executor:node1:package-health"]},"overall_summary":"All material task outcomes are satisfied. Redis is operational and demonstrably persistent across a real node reboot, inventory authentication works while unauthenticated access is denied, and the generated credential file is root-owned mode 600. Restart evidence is strong and independently corroborated by lifecycle boot identities. Hygiene is high aside from one leftover temporary reboot log.","limitations":["No separate Redis cluster-mode or cluster-state probe was captured after restart. Clustering is not part of this single-node public contract, so this does not reduce task functionality, but the provider-wide restart protocol is not literally complete on that point.","The lifecycle failed-services probe was unsupported because it used systemctl on Alpine; direct OpenRC Redis health was captured, but a system-wide failed-service inventory is unavailable."]}
